A cubic metre of copper has a mass of $9.0 \times 10^{3} \mathrm{~kg}$, and $6.0 \times 10^{23}$ copper atoms have a mass of 63.5 g, so
$$
\begin{aligned}
n & =\frac{6.0 \times 10^{23}}{63.5} \times 9.0 \times 10^{6} \\
& =8.5 \times 10^{28} \mathrm{~m}^{-3}
\end{aligned}
$$

which gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
v_{d} & =\frac{1.5}{8.5 \times 10^{28} \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times 1.0 \times 10^{-7}} \\
& =1.1 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{~m} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}=1.1 \mathrm{~mm} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& t=\frac{R_{t}-R_{0}}{R_{100}-R_{0}} \times 100, \quad R_{t}=R_{0}(1+\alpha t) \\
& =\frac{5.795-5}{5.23-5} \times 100 \\
& =\frac{0.795}{0.23} \times 100=345.65^{\circ} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}
$$

According to Ohm's law,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E & =I r \\
I & =\frac{E}{r} \\
& =\frac{12}{0.4}=30 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$

According to Ohm's law,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& V=I R \\
& =0.5 \times 17 \\
& =8.5 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}
$$

$\alpha$ is given by the relation,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \alpha=\frac{R_{1}-R}{R\left(T_{1}-T\right)} \\
& T_{1}-T=\frac{R_{1}-R}{R \alpha} \\
& T_{1}-27=\frac{117-100}{100\left(1.7 \times 10^{-4}\right)} \\
& T_{1}-27=1000 \\
& T_{1}=1027^{\circ} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}
$$

Resistance is related with the resistivity as
$$
\begin{aligned}
R & =\rho \frac{l}{A} \\
\rho & =\frac{R A}{l} \\
& =\frac{5 \times 6 \times 10^{-7}}{15}=2 \times 10^{-7} \Omega \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}
$$

It is related with temperature and resistance as
$$
\begin{aligned}
\alpha & =\frac{R_{2}-R_{1}}{R_{1}\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\right)} \\
& =\frac{2.7-2.1}{2.1(100-27.5)}=0.0039^{\circ} \mathrm{C}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

$T_{2}$ can be obtained by the relation for $\alpha$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \alpha=\frac{R_{2}-R_{1}}{R_{1}\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\right)} \\
& T_{2}-27^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=\frac{82.14-71.87}{71.87 \times 1.7 \times 10^{-4}}=840.5 \\
& T_{2}=840.5+27=867.5^{\circ} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}
$$

Current flowing in the circuit $=I$, which is given by the relation,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I & =\frac{V^{1}}{R+r} \\
& =\frac{112}{15.5+5}=\frac{112}{16}=7 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$

The relation for current using Ohm's law is,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I & =\frac{E}{R} \\
& =\frac{12}{6}=2 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, total resistance of the combination is $\frac{20}{19} \Omega$.
Emf of the battery, $V=20 \mathrm{~V}$
Current ( $I_{1}$ ) flowing through resistor $R_{1}$ is given by,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{1} & =\frac{V}{R_{1}} \\
& =\frac{20}{2}=10 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$
Current ( $I_{2}$ ) flowing through resistor $R_{2}$ is given by,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{2} & =\frac{V}{R_{2}} \\
& =\frac{20}{4}=5 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$
Current $\left(I_{3}\right)$ flowing through resistor $R_{3}$ is given by,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{3} & =\frac{V}{R_{3}} \\
& =\frac{20}{5}=4 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}
$$
Total current, $I=I_{1}+I_{2}+I_{3}=10+5+4=19 \mathrm{~A}$
Therefore, the current through each resister is $10 \mathrm{~A}, 5 \mathrm{~A}$, and 4 A respectively and the total current is 19 A.

The balance condition is given by the relation,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{E_{1}}{E_{2}} & =\frac{l_{1}}{l_{2}} \\
E_{2} & =E_{1} \times \frac{l_{2}}{l_{1}} \\
& =1.25 \times \frac{63}{35}=2.25 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}
$$

Surface charge density of the earth, $\sigma=10^{-9} \mathrm{C} \mathrm{m}^{-2}$
Current over the entire globe, $I=1800 \mathrm{~A}$
Radius of the earth, $r=6.37 \times 10^{6} \mathrm{~m}$
Surface area of the earth,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& A=4 \pi r^{2} \\
& =4 \pi \times\left(6.37 \times 10^{6}\right)^{2} \\
& =5.09 \times 10^{14} \mathrm{~m}^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
Charge on the earth surface,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& q=\sigma \times A \\
& =10^{-9} \times 5.09 \times 10^{14} \\
& =5.09 \times 10^{5} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}
$$
Time taken to neutralize the earth's surface $=t$

Current, $I=\frac{q}{t}$
$$
\begin{aligned}
t & =\frac{q}{I} \\
& =\frac{5.09 \times 10^{5}}{1800}=282.77 \mathrm{~s}
\end{aligned}
$$

The network is infinite. Hence, equivalent resistance is given by the relation,
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \therefore R^{\prime}=2+\frac{R^{\prime}}{\left(R^{\prime}+1\right)} \\
& \left(R^{\prime}\right)^{2}-2 R^{\prime}-2=0 \\
& R^{\prime}=\frac{2 \pm \sqrt{4+8}}{2} \\
& \quad=\frac{2 \pm \sqrt{12}}{2}=1 \pm \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The relation connecting emf and balance point is,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{E_{1}}{l_{1}} & =\frac{\varepsilon}{l} \\
\varepsilon & =\frac{l}{l_{1}} \times E_{1} \\
& =\frac{82.3}{67.3} \times 1.02=1.247 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}
$$
